      ":question/problem": "$\\int \\frac{30 x + 60}{(x)(x + 3)}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 3 x=(x)(x + 3).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30 x + 60}{(x)(x + 3)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x + 3}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the denominator gives\n\n$$\n30 x + 60=A(x + 3)+B(x).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B$:\n\n$$\n-30=-3B,\\qquad B=10.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A$:\n\n$$\n60=3A,\\qquad A=20.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{30 x + 60}{(x)(x + 3)}\\,\\text{d}x&=20\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+10\\int \\frac{1}{x + 3}\\,\\text{d}x\\\\&=20\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+10\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x + 3|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
